{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for severe structural underestimation: The model consistently forecasts a slow, linear drift near the current price, failing to capture the sudden, high-magnitude upward breakout (spike) observed in the true data starting around day 7-8 of the horizon. Apply a step-up adjustment to the forecast baseline once the price breaks the recent 18-day high.",
    "Eliminate excessive mean reversion bias: The model assumes prices will revert to the ~78-80 range immediately after volatility. In reality, the market exhibits strong momentum, sustaining prices above 85-90 for extended periods. Remove the downward pull in the forecast trajectory during breakout phases.",
    "Adjust for lag in trend recognition: The model's 30-day forecast is too smooth and reacts too slowly to the shift from a ~78 mean to a ~88 mean. Increase the slope of the forecast curve to match the observed momentum velocity rather than the historical average.",
    "Ignore sentiment nullity as a signal for stability: The zero sentiment history does not indicate a lack of volatility; the model incorrectly treats the flat sentiment as a signal for a flat price path. Do not rely on sentiment history to dampen volatility expectations when price action shows structural regime shifts.",
    "Reduce overshoot correction on spikes: The model fails to anticipate the magnitude of the spike (reaching 91+). Do not apply standard mean-reversion logic to cap the forecast; instead, extend the upper bound of the forecast range to accommodate sustained high-price regimes."
  ]
}
